## 1. Introduction
Antibiotics have been extensively used in recent years in human and veterinary medicine. Antibiotics have been frequently detected in final effluents of wastewater treatment plants, pharmaceutical effluents and environmental waters including underground and surface water. Many of these drugs have low biodegradability and therefore are persistent in the environment [1]. It has been reported that in China, fluoroquinolone antibiotics (FQAs) are the most widely used group of antibiotics in the treatment of serious infections in humans and domestic animals, among which norfloxacin (NOR), ciprofloxacin(CIP) and ofloxacin account for approximately $98\%$ [2]. FQAs in some surface water have been reported to be $\upmu\up g$ ${\mathrm{~L}}^{-1}$ [3], and $\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}}$ in some wastewater [2]. Unfortunately, even though trace concentrations of FQAs remaining in water are still harmful to some aquatic organisms [4]. They could also exert a high antibacterial activity and thereby increase the potential threat to human health. Therefore, effective removal of FQAs in environmental water has attracted increasing attention. Various chemical and physical methods and technologies have been developed for the removal of FQAs in water such as chemical oxidation [5], biodegradation [6], photodegradation [7] and membrane techniques [8]. Compared to other techniques, adsorption has emerged as a simple, economical and efficient method in antibiotic waste removal and does not produce toxic intermediates [9]. However, the efficiency of adsorption technology is deeply affected by the properties of adsorbents. Several adsorbent materials have been reported for the removal of FQAs from aqueous solutions, e.g., multiwall carbon nanotubes [10], activated carbons [11], graphene hydrogel [12], graphene oxide [13], birnessite [14] and magnetic molecularly imprinted chitosan [15].
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are novel functional materials consisting of various transition metal ions interconnected by a variety of organic ligands. Due to the MOFs’ unique characteristics such as enormous surface area, ultrahigh porosity and tunable pore size, their extensive potential applications have been demonstrated including gas storage [16–19], catalysis [20–22], and drug delivery [23,24]. While the use of MOFs as sorbents in gas phase is well-known, some MOFs show limitations in liquid phase adsorption due to their relatively low stability in water and their hydrophilic nature [25–28]. For the copper-based HKUST-1 MOF, the formula unit is constructed with 12 carboxylate oxygens from two benzene tricarboxylic acids (BTC) ligands that bind to four coordination sites for each of the three ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions [29]. HKUST-1 is considered to be a thermally and chemically stable MOF with a high surface area, accessible unsaturated metal-ion sites and mild synthetic conditions [30–39]. HKUST-1 has exhibited good performance in the adsorption of metal and organic pollutants such as silver nanoparticles [40], p-nitro phenol [41], and sulfonamide antibiotic [42]. However, the used pure HKUST-1 adsorbent is difficult to separate and recycle from a large volume of water, restricting its application in wastewater treatment.
Recently, magnetic materials have received considerable attention. Compared to the traditional separation technology such as filtration and centrifugation, magnetic adsorbents could be easily separated and recycled under an external magnetic field [43–45]. Several magnetic MOFs adsorbents have been reported. For example, Yang et al. [46] prepared $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 for the removal of nitroresorcinol. Wang et al. [47] synthesized $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/M I L}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ for the decolorization of acid red 1 and orange G. Ke et al. [48] reported thiol-functionalized $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{BTC})_{2}}$ core-shell magnetic microspheres which exhibited remarkably selective adsorption affinity for $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . Our group also has devoted some efforts to the preparation and application of magnetic adsorbents [49–51]. However, to the best of our knowledge, there has been no report regarding the application of magnetic MOFs in FQAs removal from aqueous solutions and achieving recyclable use.
Therefore, in this work, we have synthesized hybrid magnetic composites based on the water-stable HKUST-1 MOF, viz Fe ${}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4},$ HKUST-1, by a simple and facile method and applied them to remove the representative NOR and CIP FQAs from aqueous solutions. The adsorbents were thoroughly characterized by Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy, X-ray diffractometer (XRD), scanning/transmission electron microscopy (SEM/TEM), thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), vibrating sample magnetometry (VSM) and nitrogen adsorption-desorption analysis. Various factors influencing the adsorption process including the amount of adsorbent, solution pH and salt concentration were investigated in detail. Additionally, the adsorption kinetics, adsorption isotherms, adsorption thermodynamics, and the reusability of the Fe3O4/HKUST-1 adsorbents were explored. Furthermore, the possible adsorption reaction and mechanism of the Fe3O4/HKUST-1 for FQAs were also proposed.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Reagents and materials
Norfloxacin (NOR) and ciprofloxacin (CIP) (as shown in Fig. 1) as well asbenzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles $(20\mathrm{nm})$ were purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Chemistry Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). $\mathbf{Cu(OAc)_{2}{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ was purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Ethanol and dimethylformamide (DMF) were provided by Aibi Chemical Reagent Limited Company (Shanghai, China). Anhydrous sodium chloride was purchased from Jiangsu Powerful Function Chemical Co., Ltd. (Jiangsu, China). All solvents and reactants were at least of analytical grade. Ultrapure water (18.2 MΩ cm) used throughout the work was produced by an ultra-pure water system MingcheD- $24^{\mathrm{UV}}$ (Millipore, France).
## 2.2. Synthesis of Fe3O4/HKUST-1 and HKUST-1
The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ /HKUST-1 composites were synthesized as follows. Two grams $(2{\mathrm{~g)}}$ of $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBTC}$ was dissolved in $160~\mathrm{\mL}$ mixed solutions (DMF:ethanol, v/v, 1:1) and then transferred into a $500~\mathrm{mL}$ flask. Under stirring, $0.3\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ anhydrous ethanol was slowly added into the mixture and refluxed for $2\mathrm{h}$ at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Afterward, $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of aqueous solution of $3.3\mathrm{~g~}$ of $\mathbf{Cu(OAc)_{2}{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ was added to the above solution. This reaction mixture was refluxed at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with stirring for additional $^\textrm{\scriptsize7h}$ . After cooling to ambient temperature, the precipitates were collected by a magnet and were washed several times with ultrapure water and absolute ethyl alcohol. Finally, the product was dried overnight at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a vacuum oven. The synthesis procedure is schematically shown in Fig. 2. The pure HKUST-1 was synthesized by the same process without adding $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ .
## 2.3. Instrumentation for characterization
Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were recorded using a PerkinElmer Frontier (PerkinElmer, USA) spectrometer at the wave number range from 4000 to $400~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . X-ray diffractometer (XRD) patterns were recorded on D8 Advance (Bruker, USA). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) for thermal stability was performed using a thermogravimetric analyzer SDTQ 600 (TA, USA) at the heating rate of $10\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C\ m i n^{-1}}$ from
$50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The morphologies of the magnetic MOFs were characterized by a SUPRA 55 scanning electron microscope (SEM, ZEISS, Germany) and FEI Tecnai G2 F20 transmission electron microscope (TEM, FEI, USA). Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) was also carried out on the TEM. Magnetic hysteresis loops with an applied field from -10,000 to $10,000~\mathrm{O_{e}}$ were obtained by VSM 7307 vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM) (Lake Shore, USA) at room temperature. Nitrogen adsorption-desorption analysis was performed on a 3H-2000PS1 (BeiShiDe, China) for Brunauer-Emmet-Teller (BET) surface area. Zeta potential change was analyzed using a Zetasizer (ZetaPlus, Brookhaven).
## 2.4. Adsorption tests
The stock solutions of CIP and NOR at $100~\mathrm{{mg~L}^{-1}}$ were prepared by dissolving solid CIP and NOR, respectively, in ultrapure water. Daily used working solutions were prepared by diluting the stock solution to different concentrations with ultrapure water. The concentrations of CIP and NOR were analyzed using a high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) system (Agilent 1100) with a fluorescence detector (FLD) at the excitation wavelength of $290~\mathrm{nm}$ and the emission wavelength of $440\ \mathrm{nm}$ . An analytical column ZORBAX SB- $\mathbf{{C}}_{18}$ ( $100{\times}4.6~\mathrm{mm}$ , $5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ ) was used at room temperature. The mobile phase consisted of $85\%$ (A) methanol and $15\%$ (B) phosphoric acid- $1\%$ triethylamine $\left(\mathrm{pH}3.0\right)$ at the constant flow rate of $1.0~\mathrm{mL~min}^{-1}$ . Calibration curves were obtained for the CIP and NOR at the concentration ranging from 0.05 to $10\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ .
Several extraction parameters that influenced the adsorption efficiency, including the amount of adsorbent, adjustment of solution $\mathrm{\pH}$ and effect of salt concentration, were investigated. Then, $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of CIP or NOR $(20~\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}};$ ) solution and a certain amount of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ HKUST-1 were placed in $250~\mathrm{mL}$ conical flasks, which were shaken at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to conduct the adsorption experiments. Subsequently, the magnetic adsorbents were isolated from the solution by an external magnet.
The equilibrium concentrations of CIP and NOR were determined by HPLC-FLD. The adsorption capacity $q_{\mathrm{e}}(\mathbf{m}\mathbf{g}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1})$ was calculated based on Eq. (1),
$$
\begin{array}{r}{q_{\mathrm{e}}=\frac{(C_{0}-C_{\mathrm{e}})V}{W}}\end{array}
$$
Where $C_{0}$ and $C_{\mathrm{e}}$ mean the initial and the equilibrium concentrations of the compounds (CIP and NOR) in solutions $(\mathrm{mg~L^{\-1}})$ ), respectively, W is the mass of the adsorbent $(\mathbf{g})$ , and $V$ is the volume of the solution (L).
For adsorption kinetic studies, different concentrations (10, 20, 40, 60, $80\mathrm{\mg\L^{-1}},$ of 50 mL CIP and NOR solutions were prepared by adding $2~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ in individual flasks, and then adjusting the pH of these solutions to7.0. Each concentration of these solutions was placed in a shaker for a fixed time (from 10 to $60~\mathrm{{min}},$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The remaining concentrations of CIP and NOR were measured by HPLC-FLD.
For adsorption isotherm studies, batch adsorption experiments at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were carried out at different initial CIP and NOR concentrations ranging from $10\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ to $80\mathrm{\mgL^{-1}\left(p H=7.0\right)}$ with 2 mg of Fe3O4/HKUST-1. The final concentrations of CIP and NOR were measured by HPLC-FLD.
Thermodynamic parameters of the adsorption process were further performed at $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with other experimental conditions unaltered. The concentrations of remaining CIP and NOR in the resultant solutions were measured after $30\mathrm{min}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of Fe3O4/HKUST-1
Magnetic copper based MOFs namely $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{H}$ KUST-1composites were synthesized in a simple and facile manner, and then were characterized by FT-IR, XRD, SEM, TEM, TGA, VSM and nitrogen adsorption-desorption as described below. Fig. 3 shows the FT-IR spectra of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (a), pure HKUST-1(b) andFe ${}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ /HKUST-1 (c). In Fig. 3a, the band at $590\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ may be attributed to the Fe-O-Fe vibration of pure magnetite $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . In Fig. 3b and Fig. 3c, the bands at 1449 and $1646~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ were possibly related to -O-C-O- groups, and the bands at 1374 and $1548~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ could indicate $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ stretching, demonstrating the incorporation of BTC in the framework [30,42]. The bands approximately 3398 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ could be ascribed to the –OH of the crystalline water molecules within the Fe3O4/HKUST-1 composites. Meanwhile, the band at $680~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ indicated bonding between Cu-O, and the band at $580~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ corresponded to the Fe-O groups of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (Fig. 3c). Compared to the absorption bands of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and pure HKUST-1, it was obviously seen that HKUST-1 crystals were well-combined with the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ microspheres forming the composites.
Fig. 4 shows the XRD patterns of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (a), pure HKUST-1(b) and Fe3O4/HKUST-1 (c). Characteristic peaks in Fig. 4a at $2\uptheta=30.1^{\circ}$ , $35.5^{\circ}$ , $43.1^{\circ}$ , $57.0^{\circ}$ and $62.7^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the (220), (311), (400), (511) and (440) planes can be attributed to the crystalline structure of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , which is in accordance with that reported [42]. As shown in Fig. 4b and 4c, the diffraction peaks at $9.6^{\circ}$ (220), $11.5^{\circ}$ (222), $13.2^{\circ}$ (440), 16.3° (422), $17.3^{\circ}$ (511), $20.0^{\circ}$ (660), $29.3^{\circ}$ (751), $35.0^{\circ}$ (773) and $46.8^{\circ}$ (751) indicated the cubic lattice of the HKUST-1 [48]. The reflection peaks in XRD pattern of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ HKUST-1 matched well with the simulated pure
HKUST-1 crystals and the reported $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ microspheres.
The morphologies of the obtained $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ HKUST-1 composites were identified by SEM and TEM, as shown in Fig. 5. The TEM image of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles was shown in Fig. 5a, and the average size of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is about $20~\mathrm{nm}$ . The SEM image of pure HKUST-1in Fig. 5b shows a high crystallinity of HKUST-1, which is in a large crystal size at about $10\upmu\mathrm{m}$ [42]. The SEM image in Fig. 5c and TEM image in Fig. 5d show that the spherical $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles were assembled onto the external surface of the amorphous HKUST-1 crystals, providing direct evidence for the successful synthesis of Fe3O4/HKUST-1 composites. The average diameter of the composites was approximately $10\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Meanwhile, the EDX analysis was used to measure the atomic species present in Fe ${}_{;3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ /HKUST-1. As can be seen from Fig. S1, the elements C, O, Fe and Cu were detected, and the per weight ratio of Cu/Fe by EDX analysis was about 5:3, which could indicate the ratio of the two phases $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and HKUST-1) in the hybrid material was about 5:3 [48].
The thermal stability of the prepared composite material was examined with the temperature increasing from 50 to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . TGA curves of the three related materials including $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (a), pure HKUST-1 (b) and Fe3O4/HKUST-1 (c) were shown in Fig. 6. As seen, the decomposition process of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ HKUST-1was mainly divided into three regions, and the tendency was similar to pure HKUST-1. As shown in Fig. 6c, the weight loss of about $10\mathrm{wt}\%$ at the first stage from 50 to $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ could be ascribed to the volatilization of crystal water molecules and the release of remaining DMF; organic linkers of the framework began to degrade in the second stage from 200 to $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and the weight loss reached about $35\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ ; subsequently, slight weight loss occurred from 400 to $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and then remained almost constant with the increasing temperature higher than $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , mainly due to the collapse of HKUST-1 crystals that led to the formation of CuO [30,52,53]. Therefore, the thermal stability temperature of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ could be deduced to be $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ .The weight loss of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , pure HKUST-1 and Fe3O4/HKUST-1 composites could also confirm the mass ratio of the two phases of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and HKUST-1calculated from EDX analysis.
Fig. 7 shows the magnetization curves, suggesting the superparamagnetic characteristics of the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ composites. The saturation magnetization value of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ was 44 emu $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which was sufficient for the separation of Fe3O4/HKUST-1 from aqueous solution by the application of an external magnetic field. Additionally, the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ composites were well-dispersed in water and could be easily isolated from the aqueous solutions by a magnet (inset of Fig. 7).
Nitrogen adsorption-desorption was performed to investigate the BET surface area and pore size of samples. Fig. 8 shows the related isothermal curves of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (a), pure HKUST-1 (b) and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{H}$ KUST-1 (c); corresponding BET surface areas were calculated to be $9.8~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , 567.9 m2 g-1 and 327.9 m2 g-1, respectively. The BET surface area ratio of pure HKUST-1 $(567.9\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{\g}^{-1})$ and Fe3O4/HKUST- $1(327.9\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ) was about 5:3, which is coincident with the ratio of the two phases $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and HKUST-1) calculated by EDX analysis. The decrease of BET surface area of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ HKUST-1compared to pure HKUST-1 was likely due to the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles with low surface area. The average pore size distribution was studied using the Barret-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) method for $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , pure HKUST-1 and Fe3O4/HKUST-1, and corresponding values were calculated to be $4.43~\mathrm{nm}$ , $9.84\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $6.16\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The large pore size is quite possibly due to that the redundant DMF and water molecules in the pores of HKUST-1were evacuated by ethanol [42,54]. The CIP and NOR molecules with the longest interatomic distance of about $1.4~\mathrm{nm}$ and the minimum interatomic distances about $0.7{\mathrm{~nm}}$ (calculated with the Dmol software), could easily enter into the $6.16~\mathrm{{nm}}$ pores of the Fe3O4/HKUST-1. It is believed that adsorbent shows a good regeneration property when pore-diameter is 3–6 times larger than the adsorbate-molecule size [55]. The pore size of the Fe3O4/HKUST-1is about 4.3 times larger than the antibiotic molecules, which is beneficial to the adsorbent recycle process.
## 3.2. Adsorption properties
## 3.2.1. Effect of initial solution pH
The pH value of the solution has a significant influence on the adsorption process by changing both the form of the target adsorbates and the surface binding sites of the adsorbent. In this procedure, adsorption experiments of CIP and NOR were conducted within the solution pH ranging from 4.0 to11.0, as adjusted by $0.01\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ of HCl or NaOH solutions. As shown in Fig.9, the adsorption capacities of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ for CIP and NOR increased with the solution pH increasing from 4 to 7. However, the adsorption capacities of two antibiotics both decreased at solution pH value above 7. As seen from the composition of CIP and NOR shown in Fig. 1, the groups of CIP and NOR molecules in aqueous solutions can form three species by protonation-deprotonation reaction, including cationic species (CIP for $\mathrm{pH}<5.90\$ , NOR for $\mathrm{pH}<6.22\rangle$ , zwitterionic species (CIP for $5.90<\mathrm{pH}<8.89$ , NOR for $6.22<\mathrm{\pH}<8.51,$ ) and anionic species (CIP for $\mathrm{pH}>8.89$ , NOR for $\mathrm{pH}>8.51\$ ) [12,15]. Therefore, at $\mathrm{pH}~7.0$ , CIP and NOR mainly exist in neutral forms, as zwitter-ions with a fraction of cations. The zeta potential change of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ /HKUST-1 at solution pH varying from
4.0 to 10.0 suggested that the surface of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ /HKUST-1 adsorbent was negatively charged at $\mathrm{pH}~4.0$ to 10.0. At pH 7.0, the higher adsorption capacity is due to the electrostatic interactions between the negatively charged $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ and part of cationic antibiotics, and π-π interactions between the benzene rings within $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ HKUST-1 and antibiotics. Additionally, the Cu-O-Cu in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ /HKUST-1and -NH in the neutral form of CIP and NOR will induce the formation of hydrogen bonds, which is also beneficial for the adsorption process. For $\mathrm{pH}<7.0$ , the low adsorption capacities were mainly due to the disappearance of hydrogen bonding while -NH was protonated to $\mathrm{NH_{2}^{+}\cdot\overleftarrow{F o r}\ p H>7.0}$ , the emerging anion species of CIP and NOR enhanced the electrostatic repulsion to $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ , leading to the decrease of adsorption capacity. Therefore, rational analysis concluded that π-π interactions, hydrogen bonding and electrostatic interactions were the three important mechanisms for adsorption of the two antibiotics on Fe3O4/HKUST-1. Therefore, the solution pH was set at 7.0 in further studies.
## 3.2.2. Effect of adsorbent dosage
The amount of adsorbent could directly affect the adsorption capacity. To obtain the optimal adsorption conditions, experiments were carried out by adding1–5 mg of adsorbents to a series of $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of CIP or NOR $(20\mathrm{\mg\L^{-1}}.$ ) solutions. The adsorption capacities decreased with the removal efficiencies increase when the amount of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ increased from1 to $5~\mathrm{mg}$ , as observed in Fig. S2.This result can be explained as due to all of the adsorption sites being sufficiently exposed in the solution when adsorbent dosage was low, so that the adsorption could reach saturation in a short time, resulting in a higher adsorption capacity [56]. However, with the increased adsorbent dosage, the number of unoccupied sorption sites on the adsorbent surface was excessive compared to the fixed amount of the antibiotic molecules in the solution, leading to the decreasing of adsorption capacity. Therefore, as a good compromise between the adsorption capacity and the amount of adsorbent, the dosage of $2\:\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ HKUST-1was selected for further work.
## 3.2.3. Effect of salt concentration
The presence of salt affects the adsorption process by altering the ionic strength and viscosity of the solution. Various concentrations of NaCl (0, 0.01, 0.1, 1 molL-1) were added into the solution to adjust the salinity, with the results shown in Fig.S3. It can be seen that the adsorption capacities suddenly dropped at the salinity increased to $0.1{\mathrm{mol~L}}^{-1}$ . This phenomenon can be explained as due to the addition of NaCl inhibiting the mass transfer from the aqueous to solid phases by increasing the viscosity and the density of the solution. In addition, the dissociative NaCl surrounding the adsorption sites on the surface of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ /HKUST-1 neutralizes the surface charge of the adsorbent, impeding the electrostatic interactions between the antibiotic molecules and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ HKUST-1 [47]. Therefore, subsequent experiments were conducted without NaCl addition.
## 3.2.4. Adsorption kinetics
The adsorption rate is an important objective of our investigations. The adsorption kinetics results are shown in Fig.10. At different initial concentrations of antibiotics, the curves of adsorption capacity vs. time showed the same trend. The maximum adsorption capacities for CIP and NOR were higher for higher initial concentration. It was also evident from Fig.10that the adsorption capacities sharply increased in the first $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , and then gradually achieved equilibrium after $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Therefore, the adsorption time of $30~\mathrm{min}$ can reach the adsorption equilibrium and was chosen for further experiments.
Two kinds of kinetic models, the pseudo first-order (Eq. (2)) and pseudo second-order (Eq. (3)) models were used to estimate the rate of the adsorption process.
$$
\begin{array}{c}{{\ln(q_{\mathrm{e}}-q_{\mathrm{t}})=\ln q_{\mathrm{e}}-k_{1}t}}\\ {{{}}}\\ {{{\frac{t}{q_{\mathrm{t}}}}={\frac{1}{k_{2}q_{\mathrm{e}}^{2}}}+{\frac{1}{q_{\mathrm{e}}}}t}}\end{array}
$$
Here, $t$ is the adsorption time (min), $q_{\mathrm{e}}$ is the equilibrium absorption capacity $\mathrm{{(mgg^{-1})}}$ , $q_{\mathrm{t}}$ is the adsorption capacity at time $t~(\mathrm{mgg}^{-1})$ , and $k_{1}(\mathrm{min}^{-1})$ and $k_{2}~(\mathrm{gmg}^{-1}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1})$ are the adsorption rate constants for the pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order models, respectively.
Fig. S4 shows the plots of the pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order kinetics of the antibiotics adsorption at the CIP and NOR initial concentrations of $10{-}80\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . The kinetic parameters such as the adsorption rate constant $(k_{1},k_{2})$ and $q_{\mathrm{e}}$ could be calculated from the slope and intercept of the above equation, and their values are displayed in Table 1. The values of the correlation coefficient $R^{2}$ obtained from pseudo-first-order were in the range of 0.885–0.989 for CIP, and 0.901–0.977 for NOR. Whereas for the pseudo-second-order model, R2values were in the range of 0.997–0.999 for CIP, and 0.992–0.999 for NOR, which were higher than pseudo-first order model. Additionally, as shown in Table 1, the estimated adsorption capacities $(q_{\mathrm{e,exp}})$ of CIP and NOR agreed better with the data $(q_{\mathrm{e,cal}})$ calculated using the pseudo-second-order model. These results suggested that the adsorption behaviors of CIP and NOR onto Fe3O4/HKUST-1 can be described well by the pseudo-second-order kinetic equation. These results also indicated that chemisorptions dominated the adsorption process between the antibiotic molecules and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ /HKUST-1 adsorbent.
## 3.2.5. Adsorption isotherms
To realize the interactions between antibiotics and the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ /HKUST-1adsorbents, the adsorption isotherms experiments were conducted at $25\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , with the results shown in Fig.11.To further understand the adsorption mechanism of the Fe3O4/HKUST-1adsorbents onto the antibiotic molecules, two most frequently used isotherm models of Langmuir (Eq. (4)) and Freundlich (Eq. (5)) were selected to fit the experimental data.
$$
\begin{array}{l}{\frac{C_{\mathrm{e}}}{q_{\mathrm{e}}}=\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{L}}q_{\mathrm{m}}}+\frac{C_{\mathrm{e}}}{q_{\mathrm{m}}}}\\ {\ln q_{\mathrm{e}}=\ln K_{\mathrm{F}}+\frac{1}{\mathrm{n}}\ln C_{\mathrm{e}}}\end{array}
$$
The Langmuir isotherm is a model that presumes the adsorbates covering on the specific adsorption site of the adsorbent are found in the form of a monolayer. The linear correlation coefficients $(R^{2})$ calculated from Langmuir model were 0.994 for CIP and 0.995 for NOR. This result revealed that the adsorption of CIP and NOR onto the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ HKUST-1 was mostly monomolecular-layer adsorption. The adsorption parameters are listed in Table 2. The calculated maximum adsorption capacities of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ adsorbent towards CIP and NOR were $538~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ and $513~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ , respectively. The synthesized composites exhibited quite high adsorption capacities for these two antibiotics, which were much higher than the values for most of the materials reported in the literature for these two antibiotics.
Additionally, the dimensionless separation factor $(R_{\mathrm{L}})$ can also indicate the favorability of an adsorption process, and can be expressed by Eq. (6).
$$
\begin{array}{r}{R_{\mathrm{L}}=\frac{1}{1+K_{\mathrm{L}}C_{0}}}\end{array}
$$
where $\mathrm{C}_{0}$ is the maximum initial concentration of the adsorbate, and the parameter suggests that the isotherm is either irreversible $(R_{\mathrm{L}}=0)_{,}$ ), favorable $(0<R_{\mathrm{L}}<1)$ , favorable (linear, $R_{\mathrm{L}}=1$ ) or unfavorable ( $\ensuremath{R_{\mathrm{L}}}>1$ or $R_{\mathrm{L}}{<}0$ ). As can be seen in Fig. $S6,R_{\mathrm{L}}$ values of CIP and NOR were between 0 and 1, indicating that the adsorption process of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ was favorable for the two antibiotics studied in this work.
The Freundlich isotherm model is widely applied for modeling of multilayer adsorption on heterogeneous surfaces of adsorbents and is not restricted to the formation of the monolayer. The Freundlich parameters are also suitable for describing the adsorption performances. The linear correlation coefficients $(\mathbf{R}^{2})$ from the Freundlich model for CIP and NOR were 0.948 and 0.965 (listed in Table 2), respectively. In addition, the adsorption intensity and favorability of the sorbent are related to the Freundlich constant $(1/\mathrm{n})$ , and the n values of CIP and NOR on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ HKUST-1 were 2.44 and 2.81, falling between 2 and 10 [57], indicating that the adsorption conditions are favorable and different functional groups of the adsorbent create a heterogeneous adsorbent surface.
The linear correlation coefficients $(\boldsymbol{R}^{2})$ obtained from the fitting of the adsorption equilibrium data of CIP and NOR by Langmuir and Freundlich models were all above 0.9. Therefore, both monomolecular-layer adsorption and multilayer adsorption occurred in the adsorption process of CIP and NOR onto $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ HKUST-1.On the other hand, compared to the Freundlich model, the use of the Langmuir equation resulted in a higher value of ${\mathrm{\bfR}}^{2}$ for CIP and NOR in the entire concentration ranges, so that the Langmuir isotherm model reproduced the experimental data better, indicating that the adsorption process was mostly monomolecular-layer.
## 3.2.6. Adsorption mechanism
Elucidation of the adsorption mechanism of an adsorbent is important for identifying the fundamental characteristics of the adsorption process and for the further investigations of its commercial applicability. Based on the experiment data analysis discussed above, the mechanisms of adsorption for CIP and NOR onto the Fe ${}_{;3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ HKUST-1 adsorbents may be attributed to a combination of π-π interactions, hydrogen bonds and electrostatic interactions, as schematically shown in Fig. 12. It is noteworthy that the highest adsorption capacity was obtained at $\mathrm{pH}7.0$ where CIP and NOR exist either as neutral or as zwitterion molecules, so it is reasonable to speculate that hydrophobic interactions play an important role in the adsorption behaviors [58]. Additionally, possible interactions of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ toward CIP and NOR are attributed to the coordination bonds between the open $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ sites in HKUST-1 and COO groups in CIP and NOR zwitterion molecules, the intermolecular hydrogen bonds between Cu-O-Cu in HKUST-1 and -NH groups in CIP and NOR neutral molecules, the acid -base and hydrogen bonds interaction between the NH moiety of the FAQs and the COOH moiety in HKUST-1, and the π-π interactions between phenyl rings of HKUST-1 and the two antibiotics .Based on the zeta potential change of Fe3O4/HKUST-1 and the dissociation constants of CIP and NOR, it can be concluded that the electrostatic interactions would also play a role in the antibiotics’ adsorption onto the adsorbents, as could be proved by the decrease of the adsorption capacity of Fe ${}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ /HKUST-1at higher salinity in the solution. This is because the electrostatic interactions were impeded due to the excess dissociative-NaCl surrounding the adsorption sites on the surface of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ HKUST-1, neutralizing the surface charge of the adsorbent. All of the interactions discussed above concertedly facilitate and promote the adsorption process of the two antibiotics on the Fe3O4/HKUST-1 composites.
## 3.2.7. Adsorption thermodynamics
The thermodynamic parameters, standard free energy change (ΔG, kJ mol-1), enthalpy change $(\boldsymbol{\varDelta}\mathrm{H},\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}.$ ) and entropy change (ΔS, $\mathrm{J.mol^{-1}\mathrm{K^{-1}}}$ related to the adsorption were determined at 298, 308 and $318\mathrm{~K~}$ for 30 min. The change in ΔG is calculated with Eq. (7). In addition, ΔH and ΔS were calculated from the slope and the intercept of the plot of lnK versus 1/T using the van’t Hoff Eq. (8).
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\Delta\mathrm{G}=-R T\mathrm{ln}K}\\ {\mathrm{ln}K=\frac{\Delta S}{R}-\frac{\Delta H}{R T}}\end{array}
$$
where $T$ (K) is the temperature in Kelvin, $R$ $R~(8.314~\mathrm{J~mol^{-1}K})$ is the universal gas constant and $K(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ is the thermodynamic equilibrium constant for the adsorption process calculated from the ratio of the equilibrium adsorption capacity $(q_{\mathrm{e}})$ to the equilibrium concentration of the solution $(C_{\mathrm{e}})$ .
The linear plots of $\mathrm{ln}K$ versus $1/\mathrm{T}$ and the thermodynamic parameters are presented in Fig. S8 and Table 3. The values of $\varDelta G$ at 298 K, 308 K, and $318\mathrm{~K~}$ were -22.66, -20.87, - $\mathbf{\cdot}19.60\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for CIP and $-22.46,-20.36,-18.69\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for NOR, respectively.
The negative values of $\varDelta G$ indicated that this adsorption of CIP and NOR onto the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ /HKUST-1 composites involved a favorable and spontaneous process. The absolute value of $\varDelta G$ decreased with increasing temperature, clearly indicating that the adsorption of CIP and NOR were exothermic processes and were favored at lower temperatures. The negative values of $\varDelta H$ were $-48.67\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for CIP and $-60.03\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for NOR, revealing the exothermic adsorption process for the two antibiotics. The negative values of ΔS were $-87.55\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}\ K^{-}}$ 1for CIP and $-126.21\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}\ K^{-1}}$ for NOR, suggesting that the entropy decreased during the adsorption process, which means that the degree of freedom decreased on the binding sites of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ HKUST-1 adsorbents at the solid-solution interface.
## 3.3. Reusability of the Fe3O4/HKUST-1 adsorbent
Reusability is an important parameter for commercial applications. It indicates the cost effectiveness of an adsorbent. To investigate the possibility of reusing the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ , methanol/ammonia (99:1, v/v) solution was selected as the desorption solvent to wash the used Fe3O4/HKUST-1 adsorbents. Fig. 13 shows that the reusability efficiencies of the Fe3O4/HKUST-1 still remained high after ten successive cycles, demonstrating the high stability and good reusability of the adsorbents. The FT-IR spectra comparison of the exhausted material with initial Fe3O4/HKUST-1 was shown in Fig. S8A. As seen, the bands at 1449 and 1646 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ related to -O-C-O- groups, and the bands at 1374 and 1548 cm-1corresponding to $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ stretching, the bands at $680~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ towards Cu-O bonding can also be observed after the recycle procedure, which indicated that the chemical structures within the adsorbents were stable. Besides, the XRD patterns comparison of two materials in Fig. S8B suggested the crystalline structures of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ were not collapsed. The calculated BET surface area of the exhausted material was $323.1\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which was almost the same to the initial surface area ofFe ${}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ /HKUST-1 $(327.8~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{\Omega}$ ).
The water stability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ HKUST-1 composites might be because that the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles in hybrid material could protect the Cu sites from being attacked by water. Moreover, in aqueous solutions, water molecules around the HKUST-1 would interact with each other through hydrogen-bonding [59], thus the coordination between the water molecules and the Cu sites in HKUST-1 would be weakened, improving the water stability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ /HKUST-1 composites.
3.4. Comparison of the adsorption performance of various adsorbents for CIP and NOR
## 4. Conclusions
In the present work, the removal of two representative FQAs was studied using a magnetic MOF, Fe ${}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ /HKUST-1. The optimized adsorption conditions, including solution pH, the amount of adsorbent and salt concentration were determined. The adsorption process reached equilibrium within $30~\mathrm{min}$ , which was faster than for almost all previously reported adsorbents. The kinetics data of two antibiotics showed the best fit for the pseudo second-order model. On the other hand, the adsorption isotherm tests showed that the Langmuir model was more consistent with the experimental data. The maximum adsorption capacities calculated from the Langmuirequationwere $538\ensuremath{\stackrel{\textstyle\textmedspace}{\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}}}$ for CIP and $513~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ for NOR, respectively, much higher than the corresponding values for most of the materials reported in the literature for adsorption of these two antibiotics. The calculated thermodynamic parameters of the adsorption including the free energy, the enthalpy and the entropy suggested that the adsorption of the two antibiotics onto Fe3O4/HKUST-1 was spontaneous, exothermic and decreased the entropy. Furthermore, considering the effect of the pH, the structure of HKUST-1 and antibiotic molecules, it can be concluded that π-π interactions, hydrophobic interactions, electrostatic interactions and hydrogen bonding are the main sorption mechanisms for CIP and NOR removal from water using Fe3O4/HKUST-1. In addition, the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/H K U S T–1}$ could be efficiently regenerated and reused over ten times.
